WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.004 --> 00:00:05.155
(beep)

00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:07.905
(operatic music)

00:00:38.051 --> 00:00:41.051
(garage door opens)

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.219
- My name is Shintaro Okamoto.

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:48.240
I'm the founder and creative director of

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.112
Okamoto Studio Custom Ice.

00:00:51.112 --> 00:00:55.130
Coors Light asked me and
my team to think about

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:58.400
what different, unique
things we do with ice

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.640
and I wanted to really kind of

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.843
focus on the freezing process itself.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.370
Right now I'm sitting in my freezer,

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.807
here in my studio in Queens, New York.

00:01:10.807 --> 00:01:13.640
(classical music)

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.530
I always say not all Alaskans
played with ice, but I did.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.770
I started carving ice
with my father, I think,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.480
when I was, maybe 12-13.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.970
That's when my father first
entered an international

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.430
ice carving competition that
the city of Anchorage hosted.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.460
And I was his partner.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.822
It was a really terrible experience.

00:01:38.822 --> 00:01:43.788
My job was shoveling snow
and scrap ice all night

00:01:43.788 --> 00:01:46.560
but at the end the
sculpture was beautiful,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.640
the response was amazing.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.700
It was a really beautiful
thing that I really kinda

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.330
fell in love with.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.320
(water spraying)

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.183
Trying to get rid of
any kind of excess soil.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.426
Oh boy, it's gonna be really tricky.

00:02:02.426 --> 00:02:04.610
(tools clink)

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:05.643
Oh, I lost my ring.

00:02:06.486 --> 00:02:08.690
I thought this was going to be easy.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.993
This is gonna suck.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.553
Nope.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.693
Screen, screen.

00:02:22.445 --> 00:02:25.112
(playful music)

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.140
Change of plan.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.940
Plan A didn't work, plan B didn't work,

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.103
plan C I think is brilliant.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:35.813
If it works.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.384
Don't touch it, I don't
wanna do it, re-do it.

00:02:42.384 --> 00:02:44.640
(laughs)

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.550
I've always seen ice as
a time-based material.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.460
It's constantly changing, evolving.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.850
It's that kind of
embodiment of time passing.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.170
Freezing items inside the ice

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.480
does almost exact opposite.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.303
Where we stop the time.

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:06.790
(train rumbles)

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.790
All right so the ice's been
freezing since yesterday.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:15.030
So it grew quite a lot last night,

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:17.019
it was nice and cool I guess.

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:19.769
(chain clanking)

00:03:24.694 --> 00:03:26.277
That looks great.

00:03:26.277 --> 00:03:28.867
(scraping)

00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:30.210
(chain clanks)

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.030
We really work with
the moments of freezing

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.760
and studying the process
of how ice is formed

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.723
and how ice itself really
kind of comes to life.

00:03:40.723 --> 00:03:43.532
(saw whirs)

00:03:43.532 --> 00:03:45.717
(scraping)

00:03:45.717 --> 00:03:48.134
(soft music)

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.300
There's a really beautiful
dichotomy of the cold

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.786
trapping the best of
the summer within ice.

00:03:57.786 --> 00:03:59.320
(match strikes)

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.180
I think my father is
really chuckling every day

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.070
with the different kind
of things that we do

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.430
and challenges we face.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:10.250
It's him who really got me into ice.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.430
And it's often frustrating,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:14.650
cold,

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.840
not the most comfortable
things to work with

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.030
but the end result

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.630
is always mesmerizing

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:26.285
and I'm always looking for
something fun to do everyday.

00:04:26.285 --> 00:04:28.702
(soft music)

